provider: ollama-cloud (glm-5.3)
request: Rename the `watched` field of watchlist items to `seen` everywhere.

governor turn 1: plan -> movie, watchlist-store, watchlist, search, movie-detail  (Pure data-field rename: the `watched` field on watchlist items (WatchItem, and the same flag on Movie) becomes `seen` in every unit that touches it. Exported API names (toggleWatched, watchedCount), CSS classes and user-facing copy are deliberately unchanged - only the field is renamed.)
round 1: owners movie=ok watchlist-store=ok watchlist=rejected search=ok movie-detail=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 2: plan -> watchlist  (All other units applied the watched->seen rename and the oracle implicates only the watchlist unit, whose round-1 submission was rejected solely because it included an unapproved test edit. That edit (asserting the renamed .seen field) is exactly what the request calls for, so I approve it and re-instruct watchlist to resubmit its template, logic, and test changes.)
round 2: owners watchlist=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 3: plan -> watchlist-store  (tsc is green and all 37 owner tests pass except the 3 failures, which are all in the governor's own acceptance test src/acceptance/watchlist-store.spec.ts. That test constructed WatchlistStore with plain `new`, but the store's constructor registers an Angular effect(), which requires an injection context (NG0203) — so the store must be created through TestBed. The rename itself is complete and verified; no production unit needs to change. I am replacing only the acceptance test with a TestBed-based version carrying identical expectations.)
round 3: owners watchlist-store=ok; oracle GREEN
governor turn 4: accept  (The watched->seen rename is implemented across movie, watchlist-store, and watchlist; tsc is clean and all 37 tests (including the acceptance tests asserting the seen field and seen-keyed persistence) pass.)

